      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "This is very impractical and would make world creation a nightmare for world authors, and that’s not even to mention the performance impact. Maybe you could do something like this with avatars, but even that is a stretch. Each avatar would need to have colliders set up for all their limbs and body parts or use performance heavy mesh collision, and then you run into odd networking issues like determining where the collision of two people swiping at each other occurred with both having latency so their limbs would keep rubber banding into position when the actual determined collision is sent to each client. The servers would most likely have to determine these collisions to network them, but that means servers would need to each have a much higher performance capacity to actually make all these collision calculations.\n\nMost importantly though, Boneworks and Bonelabs are built on a custom game engine that had these sorts of collisions  as a core feature built in from the ground up. VRChat did not. That’s not to say Bonelabs is better, because it couldn’t handle as many people together in one world as VRChat can. It’s not about one being better then the other, instead it comes down to two different games built with two different goals in mind.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
