      "value": "@{60411ec5cc01c21febdcbe37|full_name}: and that is exactly why it would need to be calculated server side. In the diving board example, if you went to push her and both clients ran their own collision calculations on data effected by the differences in latency and both of you being in slightly different positions according to each of your clients, you would both get different outcomes which is a big problem when it comes to actual player position and your  networked Tracked IK positions. This doesn’t matter when it comes to physbones because who cares if the position of someone’s cat ear is desynced between clients, but if these collisions effect your entire player position, if you can move or not, and whether you can move your tracked body parts somewhere, then that’s a lot more important to be properly synced between users because otherwise you will have clients telling each other two different sets of information and rubber banding as they both try to conform to each other’s story of events.\n\nAlso, server side calculations aren’t some weird crazy idea. In fact a huge amount of games do this. That’s how in those games you can run into each other, collide with each other, and the collision is smooth, not jumping you back into positions or moving you in weird ways, because the server is doing all the calculations for you and sending both players the final positions. Your just telling the server your inputs.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
